{"name":"Chapel Wall","hex":"#ede2ac","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chapel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place of worship, smaller than or subordinate to a church.","A place of worship in another building or within a civil institution such as a larger church, airport, prison, monastery, school, etc.; often primarily for private prayer."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Describing a person who attends a nonconformist chapel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chapel"},{"term":"wall","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rampart of earth, stones etc. built up for defensive purposes.","A structure built for defense surrounding a city, castle etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To enclose with, or as if with, a wall or walls.","To use a wallhack."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wall"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chapel","blocks":[{"text":"A chapel is a Christian place of prayer and worship that is usually relatively small. The term has several meanings. First, smaller spaces inside a church that have their own altar are often called chapels; the Lady chapel is a common type of these. Second, a chapel is a place of worship, sometimes interfaith, that is part of a building, complex, or vessel with some other main purpose, such as a school, college, hospital, palace or large aristocratic house, castle, barracks, prison, funeral home, cemetery, hotel, airport, or military or commercial ship. Third, chapels are small places of worship, built as satellite sites by a church or monastery, for example in remote areas; these are often called a chapel of ease. A feature of all these types is that often no clergy are permanently resident or specifically attached to the chapel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/St_Paul%27s_Cathedral_Chapel_of_St_Michael_%26_St_George%2C_London_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-St_Paul%27s_Cathedral_Chapel_of_St_Michael_%26_St_George%2C_London_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapel","wikidata":"Q108325"},{"title":"Wall","blocks":[{"text":"A wall is a structure and a surface that defines an area; carries a load; provides security, shelter, or soundproofing; or serves a decorative purpose. There are various types of walls, including border barriers between countries, brick walls, defensive walls in fortifications, and retaining walls that hold back dirt, stone, water, or noise. Walls can also be found in buildings, where they support roofs, floors, and ceilings, enclose spaces, and provide shelter and security."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Noel_2005_P%C3%A9kin_031_muraille_de_chine_Mutianyu.jpg/330px-Noel_2005_P%C3%A9kin_031_muraille_de_chine_Mutianyu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wall","wikidata":"Q42948"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Apsidal_chapels.png/500px-Apsidal_chapels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Apsidal_chapels.png/1280px-Apsidal_chapels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Apsidal_chapels.png/500px-Apsidal_chapels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schematic rendering of typical \"side chapels\" in the apse of a cathedral, surrounding the ambulatory","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApsidal_chapels.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG/500px-Crviz_2015_jun_pic_25.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Crviz_2015_j